  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThe trend of lowering PoPs and QPF tonight into Sunday will\ncontinue with this update. The remnants of Hurricane Isaias are\ncurving to the northeast sooner, and the westward expansion of\nthe precip shield will only be into our CWA this evening, with\ndry air dissipating the precip potential. There is solid\nagreement in the CAMs on that scenario (HRRR, RAP, HREF, and \nRRFS). The primary forecast solution adopts a consensus blend \nof the HREF and HRRR, keeping low-end precipitation chances \n(2030%) restricted to far southeast Illinois while central \nIllinois remains dry.\n\nGlobal deterministic models (GFS, ECMWF, ECMWF-AI) and their \nrespective ensemble suites (GEFS, LREF) are in tight alignment \nregarding an unseasonably strong thermal ridge peaking on \nMonday, October 12 and now extending into Tuesday. NBM guidance\nindicates high confidence in high temperatures reaching 88F to\n92F on Monday and that same temp range south of I-72 on\nTuesday, which will challenge daily record highs across central\nIllinois climate sites.\n\nFor Tuesday night through Thursday, minor timing discrepancies \nexist between the GFS and ECMWF/ECMWF-AI. The GFS is slightly \nfaster with cold front progression on Tuesday, whereas the \nECMWF and ECMWF-AI hold the frontal zone nearly stationary \nparallel to upper-level southwesterly flow through Wednesday \nnight. DESI LREF ensemble probabilities highlight a 6075% \nchance of cumulative rainfall exceeding 1.50 inches across \ncentral and east-central Illinois. Heavy rainfall potential is \nsupported by PWAT values exceeding 1.6 inches (above the 90th \npercentile for mid-October). SPC convective outlooks, backed by\nCIPS and CSU machine learning severe probability guidance, \nindicate some risk for severe thunderstorms along and ahead of \nthe boundary on Wednesday, but mainly just west of our CWA. WPC\nExcessive Rainfall Outlooks (ERO) highlight a Marginal to Slight\nRisk for flash flooding. \n\nFollowing frontal passage Thursday night, a high-amplitude \nCanadian upper trough digs into the central and eastern United \nStates. Ensemble guidance displays remarkable consistency \nregarding an unseasonably cold airmass intruding into the \nregion. 850 mb temperatures drop into the -2C to -8C range by\nSaturday, October 17. That could support patchy frost \ndeveloping in low lying areas Saturday night. A better chance\nfor patchy frost looks to come next Sunday night.",
